window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"A Philosophy of Software Design","section":"Software design is the fight against complexity.","tool":"home","text":"Software design is the fight against complexity. A sharp, crisp tour of John Ousterhout's A Philosophy of Software Design — not a re-telling, just the ideas that move the needle. Every concept is given twice: a plain-English analogy, then the precise version, with short Python examples and before→af","keywords":["software","design","is","the","fight","against","complexity"]},{"url":"index.html#big-idea","page":"A Philosophy of Software Design","section":"The whole book in one sentence","tool":"home","text":"01 The whole book in one sentence The greatest limit on what we build is our ability to understand what we've built — so the job of design is to minimise complexity , continually. In plain English analogy Complexity is clutter in a workshop. No single stray tool stops you, but let it pile up and eve","keywords":["the","whole","book","in","one","sentence"]},{"url":"index.html#the-map","page":"A Philosophy of Software Design","section":"The map","tool":"home","text":"02 The map Complexity — the enemy the problem · 2 lessons What complexity is, how you feel it (change amplification, cognitive load, unknown unknowns), and why strategic beats tactical. Start here Deep modules — the core move the heart · 5 lessons Deep vs shallow, information hiding, general-purpose","keywords":["the","map"]},{"url":"index.html#progress","page":"A Philosophy of Software Design","section":"Your progress","tool":"home","text":"03 Your progress 0 of 12 lessons complete Reset progress See also lld guide These ideas pair well with object-oriented & low-level design. When a lesson overlaps, you'll find a “see also” link into the Design Field Guide (OOP, SOLID, patterns).","keywords":["your","progress"]},{"url":"complexity/what-is-complexity.html","page":"What is complexity?","section":"What is complexity?","tool":"complexity","text":"What is complexity? Ousterhout's whole book rests on one claim: the greatest limit on what we can build in software is our ability to understand the systems we create. Complexity is the name for everything that fights that understanding — and the entire craft of design is the fight to keep it down.","keywords":["what","is","complexity"]},{"url":"complexity/what-is-complexity.html#the-idea","page":"What is complexity?","section":"A working definition","tool":"complexity","text":"01 A working definition Complexity is anything about the structure of a system that makes it hard to understand or modify . In plain English analogy A complex codebase is a messy kitchen . You can still cook in it — but every dish takes longer, you knock things over reaching for a pan, and a newcome","keywords":["working","definition"]},{"url":"complexity/what-is-complexity.html#symptoms","page":"What is complexity?","section":"The three symptoms","tool":"complexity","text":"02 The three symptoms You know complexity by how it feels when you work. Ousterhout names three tells: Change amplification A conceptually simple change forces edits in many places. “Just recolor the banner” → the same hex code is pasted in 40 templates. Cognitive load How much you must hold in your","keywords":["the","three","symptoms"]},{"url":"complexity/what-is-complexity.html#causes","page":"What is complexity?","section":"Two root causes","tool":"complexity","text":"03 Two root causes All three symptoms grow from two underlying causes: Dependencies When a piece of code can't be understood or changed in isolation — it's entangled with others. Some dependencies are essential; design is about keeping them simple and obvious , not eliminating them. Obscurity When i","keywords":["two","root","causes","Obscure","what","is","timezone","it","inclusive","if","now","created","expire","session","Obvious","the","knowledge","in","name","and","type","ONE_DAY","timedelta","days"]},{"url":"complexity/what-is-complexity.html#incremental","page":"What is complexity?","section":"Complexity is incremental","tool":"complexity","text":"04 Complexity is incremental Complexity isn't one catastrophe — it's the accumulation of dozens of small chunks , each of which seemed harmless. No single dependency or vague name sinks a project. But each adds a grain; comprehensibility erodes a grain at a time until no one fully understands the sy","keywords":["complexity","is","incremental"]},{"url":"complexity/what-is-complexity.html#check","page":"What is complexity?","section":"Check yourself","tool":"complexity","text":"05 Check yourself Which symptom of complexity is the most dangerous? Change amplification — too many files to edit. Cognitive load — too much to keep in your head. Unknown unknowns — you can't tell what needs changing. Unknown unknowns. With the first two you at least know the work in front of you. ","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"complexity/strategic-vs-tactical.html","page":"Strategic vs tactical programming","section":"Strategic vs tactical programming.","tool":"complexity","text":"Strategic vs tactical programming. Most complexity is self-inflicted, accumulated by a mindset that treats “it works” as the finish line. Ousterhout's antidote is a mindset shift: your real job isn't to produce working code — it's to produce a great design that also happens to work.","keywords":["strategic","vs","tactical","programming"]},{"url":"complexity/strategic-vs-tactical.html#working-code-isnt-enough","page":"Strategic vs tactical programming","section":"Working code isn't enough","tool":"complexity","text":"01 Working code isn't enough If you only ever optimise for “make it work now,” you guarantee a complex system. Working code is the floor, not the goal. In plain English analogy It's the difference between paying down debt and maxing out the card . A tactical hack borrows speed today at interest — ev","keywords":["working","code","isn","enough"]},{"url":"complexity/strategic-vs-tactical.html#two-mindsets","page":"Strategic vs tactical programming","section":"Two mindsets","tool":"complexity","text":"02 Two mindsets Tactical Goal: get this feature/fix done as fast as possible. Each shortcut is tiny — a special case here, a duplicated block there. They feel harmless and ship quickly. Strategic Goal: working code and a clean design. You invest continually in structure, so the system stays easy to ","keywords":["two","mindsets"]},{"url":"complexity/strategic-vs-tactical.html#refactor","page":"Strategic vs tactical programming","section":"Same feature, two mindsets","tool":"complexity","text":"03 Same feature, two mindsets Watch tactical creep: each promo is “just one more if .” The strategic version pays a small design cost once, then absorbs new promos for free. Before · tactical def price(cart): total = sum(i.price for i in cart.items) # promos bolted on, one if at a time if cart.code ","keywords":["same","feature","two","mindsets","if","def","price","cart","total","sum","for","in","items","promos","bolted","on","one","at","time","code","SAVE10","FREESHIP","and","is_member","BOGO","cheapest","min","return","every","new","promo","edits","this","function","risks","the","others","class","Promo","Protocol"]},{"url":"complexity/strategic-vs-tactical.html#the-investment","page":"Strategic vs tactical programming","section":"The investment mindset","tool":"complexity","text":"04 The investment mindset Spend a little design effort continually — Ousterhout's rule of thumb is roughly 10–20% extra — and treat it as an investment, not a tax. The payoff isn't linear, because complexity compounds. Tactical teams feel faster for the first few weeks and are crawling within months","keywords":["the","investment","mindset"]},{"url":"complexity/strategic-vs-tactical.html#watch-it-compound","page":"Strategic vs tactical programming","section":"Watch it compound","tool":"complexity","text":"05 Watch it compound Same team, same eight change requests, two mindsets. Chip width is the days each feature cost ; the meter is the complexity left behind. Step through and watch for the crossover. Prev &#9654; Play Next Reset /-> step · Space plays · the numbers are illustrative — the shape of th","keywords":["watch","it","compound"]},{"url":"complexity/strategic-vs-tactical.html#check","page":"Strategic vs tactical programming","section":"Check yourself","tool":"complexity","text":"06 Check yourself A teammate ships features twice as fast as anyone, leaving small hacks everywhere. Is that good? Yes — shipping velocity is what matters most. No — that's a tactical tornado; their speed creates drag for everyone else. Yes, as long as the tests pass. It's a tactical tornado. Indivi","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"modules/deep-modules.html","page":"Deep modules","section":"Deep modules.","tool":"modules","text":"Deep modules. This is the central idea of the whole book. A module is any unit with an interface and an implementation — a function, a class, a service. The best modules are deep : a simple interface hiding a lot of power. Get this one habit and most other rules follow.","keywords":["deep","modules"]},{"url":"modules/deep-modules.html#interface-vs-implementation","page":"Deep modules","section":"Interface vs implementation","tool":"modules","text":"01 Interface vs implementation A module's interface is everything a user must know to use it. Its implementation is how it actually works. A good abstraction hides the second behind a small first. In plain English analogy A deep module is a microwave : one button (“+30s”) commands a magnetron, timer","keywords":["interface","vs","implementation"]},{"url":"modules/deep-modules.html#deep-vs-shallow","page":"Deep modules","section":"Deep vs shallow","tool":"modules","text":"02 Deep vs shallow Picture a module as a rectangle. The top edge is the interface (its cost — what you must learn); the area is the functionality (its benefit). You want much more area than edge . Cost = interface · Benefit = functionality Deep: a small interface over deep functionality — high benef","keywords":["deep","vs","shallow","open","read","write","close","lseek"]},{"url":"modules/deep-modules.html#refactor","page":"Deep modules","section":"Make a shallow class deep","tool":"modules","text":"03 Make a shallow class deep Getters and setters are the purest shallow interface: they expose the implementation one field at a time and push the rules onto every caller. A deep method swallows the rule. Before · shallow class Cursor: def get_line(self): return self._line def set_line(self, n): sel","keywords":["make","shallow","class","deep","Cursor","def","get_line","self","return","_line","set_line","get_col","_col","set_col","every","caller","must","know","the","rule","new","line","resets","column","cur","move_to_next_line","lives","here","once","callers","just","say","what","they","mean"]},{"url":"modules/deep-modules.html#classitis","page":"Deep modules","section":"Beware classitis","tool":"modules","text":"04 Beware classitis Red flag — classitis gotcha The belief that classes/methods should be small , taken to an extreme, breeds lots of tiny shallow classes. Each adds interface to learn and glue code to write, while hiding almost nothing. “More, smaller units” is not automatically simpler — depth is ","keywords":["beware","classitis"]},{"url":"modules/deep-modules.html#check","page":"Deep modules","section":"Check yourself","tool":"modules","text":"05 Check yourself Which interface is deeper? Five Unix calls (open/read/write/close/lseek) that hide all of disk I/O. A class with a getter and setter for each of its eight fields. Whichever has more methods — more API means more capability. The Unix calls. Depth is benefit-over-cost: a tiny interfa","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"modules/information-hiding.html","page":"Information hiding & leakage","section":"Information hiding & leakage.","tool":"modules","text":"Information hiding & leakage. Depth comes from hiding . Each module should bury a design decision — a piece of knowledge — behind its interface, so the rest of the system never has to know it. When the same knowledge shows up in two modules, you have a leak , and a dependency that will bite.","keywords":["information","hiding","leakage"]},{"url":"modules/information-hiding.html#the-idea","page":"Information hiding & leakage","section":"Hide a design decision","tool":"modules","text":"01 Hide a design decision A well-designed module encapsulates one piece of knowledge — a format, an algorithm, a policy — so changing that decision touches only that module. In plain English analogy A vending machine hides its whole mechanism behind two buttons. You don't know — or care — how it rou","keywords":["hide","design","decision"]},{"url":"modules/information-hiding.html#leakage","page":"Information hiding & leakage","section":"Information leakage","tool":"modules","text":"02 Information leakage Leakage is when one design decision is reflected in multiple modules , coupling them. The tell: you change one module and a “separate” one must change in lockstep. If two classes both know the on-disk layout of a file, the format has leaked across both — they're secretly one d","keywords":["information","leakage"]},{"url":"modules/information-hiding.html#refactor","page":"Information hiding & leakage","section":"Plug the leak","tool":"modules","text":"03 Plug the leak Splitting by execution order gives a reader and a writer that both hard-code the format. Splitting by knowledge gives one module that owns it. Before · leaky # format knowledge duplicated in BOTH classes class ConfigReader: def load(self, path): cfg = {} for line in open(path): key,","keywords":["plug","the","leak","format","knowledge","duplicated","in","BOTH","classes","class","ConfigReader","def","load","self","path","cfg","for","line","open","key","val","split","strip","return","ConfigWriter","save","with","as","items","write","again","change","to","must","edit","two","sync","one","module","OWNS"]},{"url":"modules/information-hiding.html#how-much","page":"Information hiding & leakage","section":"What to hide","tool":"modules","text":"04 What to hide Good things to bury behind an interface: file/wire formats, choice of data structure, an algorithm, units and encodings, the existence of a cache, retry/backoff policy. The test: could I change this decision later without any other module noticing? If not, the knowledge has leaked. S","keywords":["what","to","hide"]},{"url":"modules/information-hiding.html#check","page":"Information hiding & leakage","section":"Check yourself","tool":"modules","text":"05 Check yourself You change a date format in one module and three others break. What happened? Information leakage — the format decision was reflected in all four modules. Good encapsulation — the change propagated automatically. Nothing unusual; coupled changes are normal. Leakage. One design deci","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"modules/general-purpose-modules.html","page":"General-purpose modules are deeper","section":"General-purpose modules are deeper.","tool":"modules","text":"General-purpose modules are deeper. Counter-intuitively, a slightly general interface is usually simpler than a narrowly special-purpose one — and deeper. The trick is “somewhat general-purpose”: make the interface general enough to serve several uses, even while the implementation handles only what","keywords":["general","purpose","modules","are","deeper"]},{"url":"modules/general-purpose-modules.html#the-idea","page":"General-purpose modules are deeper","section":"“Somewhat” general-purpose","tool":"modules","text":"01 “Somewhat” general-purpose Aim for an interface that is general enough to support multiple uses , but don't build machinery for futures you can't see. In plain English analogy Put a power outlet in the wall, not a lamp soldered to the wires. The outlet is more general and simpler — one socket ser","keywords":["somewhat","general","purpose"]},{"url":"modules/general-purpose-modules.html#the-text-example","page":"General-purpose modules are deeper","section":"Ousterhout's text-editor example","tool":"modules","text":"02 Ousterhout's text-editor example A text class can expose one special method per UI gesture — backspace , delete_selection , insert_char — each baking in cursor/selection rules. Or it can expose two general primitives. The general pair is fewer methods, deeper, and stops the cursor knowledge from ","keywords":["ousterhout","text","editor","example","backspace","delete_selection","insert_char","class","Text","shallow","method","per","UI","gesture","each","one","now","KNOWS","about","cursors","and","selections","def","self","cursor","sel","ch","delete_to_end_of_line","new","add","another","here","insert","pos","two","delete","start","end","primitives","the"]},{"url":"modules/general-purpose-modules.html#three-questions","page":"General-purpose modules are deeper","section":"Three questions to find the sweet spot","tool":"modules","text":"03 Three questions to find the sweet spot Before settling an interface, Ousterhout asks: Simplest interface? What's the simplest interface that still covers all my current needs? (Fewer methods and general usually win together.) How many uses? In how many situations will this be used? If a method se","keywords":["three","questions","to","find","the","sweet","spot"]},{"url":"modules/general-purpose-modules.html#check","page":"General-purpose modules are deeper","section":"Check yourself","tool":"modules","text":"04 Check yourself Why is a general-purpose interface often simpler than a special-purpose one? A few general primitives replace many special methods and stop caller knowledge from leaking in. Because it has more configuration options for the future. It isn't — special-purpose is always simpler. Fewe","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"modules/different-layer-different-abstraction.html","page":"Different layer, different abstraction","section":"Different layer, different abstraction.","tool":"modules","text":"Different layer, different abstraction. Software is built in layers, and every layer should offer a different abstraction than the ones above and below it. When two adjacent layers look the same, one of them probably isn't earning its keep — the symptom is a pass-through .","keywords":["different","layer","abstraction"]},{"url":"modules/different-layer-different-abstraction.html#the-idea","page":"Different layer, different abstraction","section":"Each layer must add value","tool":"modules","text":"01 Each layer must add value If a layer's interface is similar to the layer beneath it , that's a red flag: the layer isn't providing a new abstraction. In plain English analogy A good chain of command has each rank adding judgment . A manager who just forwards every email unchanged to their boss ad","keywords":["each","layer","must","add","value"]},{"url":"modules/different-layer-different-abstraction.html#pass-through-methods","page":"Different layer, different abstraction","section":"Pass-through methods","tool":"modules","text":"02 Pass-through methods A pass-through method does little but call another method with roughly the same signature. It inflates the interface (one more thing to learn and maintain) while adding zero abstraction. Either remove it, or make it earn its place by providing something the layer below doesn'","keywords":["pass","through","methods","class","UserService","def","__init__","self","repo","get_user","uid","adds","nothing","return","save_user","user","the","service","is","hollow","layer","same","abstraction","as","one","level","up","cache","NEW","lacks","transparent","caching","of","reads","if","not","in","you","can","name"]},{"url":"modules/different-layer-different-abstraction.html#pass-through-variables","page":"Different layer, different abstraction","section":"Pass-through variables","tool":"modules","text":"03 Pass-through variables The same smell appears with data. A pass-through variable is threaded through a long chain of methods just to reach a deep one — every method in between must declare and forward a parameter it never uses. That's broad, shallow coupling: add one variable and you edit the who","keywords":["pass","through","variables"]},{"url":"modules/different-layer-different-abstraction.html#check","page":"Different layer, different abstraction","section":"Check yourself","tool":"modules","text":"04 Check yourself You find a service method that just calls the repository method with identical arguments. What is it? A pass-through method — remove it or make the layer add a new abstraction. Good layering — every call should go through a service. A performance optimization. A pass-through. Routi","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"modules/pull-complexity-down.html","page":"Pull complexity down · together or apart","section":"Pull complexity down · together or apart.","tool":"modules","text":"Pull complexity down · together or apart. Two closely-related judgment calls. First: when complexity is unavoidable, who absorbs it — the module or its users? Pull it down , into the module. Second: should two pieces of code live together or apart? A few clear tests decide.","keywords":["pull","complexity","down","together","or","apart"]},{"url":"modules/pull-complexity-down.html#pull-down","page":"Pull complexity down · together or apart","section":"Pull complexity downward","tool":"modules","text":"01 Pull complexity downward A simple interface matters more than a simple implementation. When in doubt, let the module's author suffer the complexity so its many users don't have to. In plain English analogy A great restaurant hides the chaos of the kitchen. You say “the salmon,” not “sear at 200°C","keywords":["pull","complexity","downward"]},{"url":"modules/pull-complexity-down.html#config-trap","page":"Pull complexity down · together or apart","section":"The configuration-parameter trap","tool":"modules","text":"02 The configuration-parameter trap Config parameters are often complexity pushed upward : instead of solving a problem, the module hands it to the caller — who usually has less context to choose well. Prefer to compute a sensible default inside, and let callers override only when they truly need to","keywords":["the","configuration","parameter","trap","def","make_client","host","port","timeout","retries","backoff","pool_size","keepalive","every","caller","must","choose","seven","values","they","don","understand","and","can","get","right","client","api","443","True","overrides","cfg","dict","good","defaults","update","escape","hatch","common","case"]},{"url":"modules/pull-complexity-down.html#together-or-apart","page":"Pull complexity down · together or apart","section":"Better together or better apart?","tool":"modules","text":"03 Better together or better apart? When deciding whether two chunks belong in one module or two, Ousterhout's tests are concrete: Bring together if… they share information; they're (nearly) always used together; combining simplifies the interface; or it removes duplication. Keep apart if… doing so ","keywords":["better","together","or","apart"]},{"url":"modules/pull-complexity-down.html#check","page":"Pull complexity down · together or apart","section":"Check yourself","tool":"modules","text":"04 Check yourself Your API requires callers to pass timeout , retries , and backoff on every call. What's the better design? Compute sensible defaults inside the module; allow overrides for the rare expert. Keep them required — callers know their situation best. Add even more parameters so it's full","keywords":["check","yourself","timeout","retries","backoff"]},{"url":"craft/define-errors-out-of-existence.html","page":"Define errors out of existence","section":"Define errors out of existence.","tool":"craft","text":"Define errors out of existence. Exception handling is one of the worst sources of complexity: every error you throw is a new path each caller must understand, write, and test. Ousterhout's most contrarian advice is to reduce the number of places that throw — often by redefining the semantics so the ","keywords":["define","errors","out","of","existence"]},{"url":"craft/define-errors-out-of-existence.html#exceptions-are-complexity","page":"Define errors out of existence","section":"Every exception is a path","tool":"craft","text":"01 Every exception is a path Exceptions multiply control-flow paths. The best handler is the one you never have to write because the error can't occur. In plain English analogy You can put up a “Mind the step” sign (make every visitor handle the hazard) — or you can remove the step so the floor is f","keywords":["every","exception","is","path"]},{"url":"craft/define-errors-out-of-existence.html#four-techniques","page":"Define errors out of existence","section":"Four ways to cut exceptions","tool":"craft","text":"02 Four ways to cut exceptions Define them away Redefine the API so the “error” case is just a normal result. Unix lets you delete an open file — no error. Python's list[5:99] returns what exists instead of throwing. Mask low Handle the exception at a low level and recover, so higher levels never se","keywords":["four","ways","to","cut","exceptions","list"]},{"url":"craft/define-errors-out-of-existence.html#refactor","page":"Define errors out of existence","section":"Redefine the “error” as normal","tool":"craft","text":"03 Redefine the “error” as normal A lookup that throws on a missing key forces a try/except at every call site. Redefining “missing” as “return the default” deletes that whole class of handling. Before · throws def get_config(cfg, key): if key not in cfg: raise KeyError(key) return cfg[key] # every ","keywords":["redefine","the","error","as","normal","def","get_config","cfg","key","if","not","in","raise","KeyError","return","every","caller","pays","tax","try","timeout","except","DEFAULT_TIMEOUT","default","None","get","missing","one","path","no","handler","nothing","to","forget"]},{"url":"craft/define-errors-out-of-existence.html#check","page":"Define errors out of existence","section":"Check yourself","tool":"craft","text":"04 Check yourself A method throws NotFound whenever a key is absent, and absence is common. Best fix? Redefine the API to return a default — make “absent” a normal outcome. Wrap every call in try/except so nothing crashes. Add a bare except: that ignores all errors. Define it away. If absence is com","keywords":["check","yourself","NotFound","except"]},{"url":"craft/comments-why-and-what.html","page":"Comments: why & what","section":"Comments: why & what.","tool":"craft","text":"Comments: why & what. Comments aren't a failure of code — they're how you record the things code can't express: the units, the contract, the reason. The goal of a comment is to put information that was in the designer's head into a form the next reader can recover. Good ones lower complexity by kill","keywords":["comments","why","what"]},{"url":"craft/comments-why-and-what.html#why","page":"Comments: why & what","section":"Why comments exist","tool":"craft","text":"01 Why comments exist Comments capture knowledge that cannot be expressed in the code itself — intent, rationale, units, invariants, contracts. In plain English analogy Code is the recipe steps ; a comment is the chef's margin note — “this resting step is what keeps it from going rubbery.” The steps","keywords":["why","comments","exist"]},{"url":"craft/comments-why-and-what.html#what-not-to-write","page":"Comments: why & what","section":"Different level, not the same level","tool":"craft","text":"02 Different level, not the same level A useful comment is at a different level of detail than the code — either lower (precision: units, edge cases, what None means) or higher (intuition: what this does and why). A comment that restates the code adds noise and rots. Restates the code i += 1 # add o","keywords":["different","level","not","the","same","None","add","one","to","retries","are","capped","at","beyond","that","upstream","rate","limits","us"]},{"url":"craft/comments-why-and-what.html#refactor","page":"Comments: why & what","section":"From noise to value","tool":"craft","text":"03 From noise to value Before · noise def poll(timeout): # set the deadline deadline = now() + timeout # loop until deadline while now() < deadline: # check readiness if ready(): return True # return true # return false return False After · value def poll(timeout): \"\"\"Wait up to `timeout` SECONDS fo","keywords":["from","noise","to","value","def","poll","timeout","set","the","deadline","now","loop","until","while","check","readiness","if","ready","return","True","true","false","False","Wait","up","SECONDS","for","Returns","as","soon","else","at","Busy","spins","no","sleep","sub","ms","latency","pass"]},{"url":"craft/comments-why-and-what.html#comments-first","page":"Comments: why & what","section":"Write the comment first","tool":"craft","text":"04 Write the comment first Writing the interface comment before the implementation turns commenting into a design tool . If the comment is long or awkward, the abstraction is too complex — fix the design, not the prose. As a bonus, comments written first stay in sync because they shaped the code. Re","keywords":["write","the","comment","first"]},{"url":"craft/comments-why-and-what.html#check","page":"Comments: why & what","section":"Check yourself","tool":"craft","text":"05 Check yourself Which comment is worth writing? count = 0 # set count to zero # offset is in bytes from the start of the frame, not the packet # this is a function The units/contract comment. It records something the code genuinely cannot express (which origin, which unit) — exactly the obscurity ","keywords":["check","yourself","count","set","to","zero","offset","is","in","bytes","from","the","start","of","frame","not","packet","this","function"]},{"url":"craft/names-consistency-obvious.html","page":"Names, consistency & obvious code","section":"Names, consistency & obvious code.","tool":"craft","text":"Names, consistency & obvious code. Three habits that decide whether reading your code is effortless or exhausting. Precise names create an accurate picture in one glance; consistency lets readers reason by analogy; and the sum of both is obvious code — understood quickly, correctly, without puzzling","keywords":["names","consistency","obvious","code"]},{"url":"craft/names-consistency-obvious.html#names","page":"Names, consistency & obvious code","section":"Names are tiny abstractions","tool":"craft","text":"01 Names are tiny abstractions A good name is precise : it forms an accurate image and rules out wrong meanings. Vague names ( data , tmp , obj , do_it ) are obscurity in disguise. In plain English analogy Names are the labels on moving boxes . “Stuff” forces you to open every box; “Kitchen — fragil","keywords":["names","are","tiny","abstractions","data","tmp","obj","do_it"]},{"url":"craft/names-consistency-obvious.html#refactor","page":"Names, consistency & obvious code","section":"Precise names make code obvious","tool":"craft","text":"02 Precise names make code obvious Before · obscure def proc(d, t): r = [] for x in d: if x[1] > t: r.append(x[0]) return r # what is d? t? x[1]? you must decode it all After · obvious def names_above(scores, threshold): \"\"\"scores: list of (name, value). Returns the names whose value exceeds thresho","keywords":["precise","names","make","code","obvious","def","proc","for","in","if","append","return","what","is","you","must","decode","it","all","names_above","scores","threshold","list","of","name","value","Returns","the","whose","exceeds","reads","like","sentence","implements"]},{"url":"craft/names-consistency-obvious.html#consistency","page":"Names, consistency & obvious code","section":"Consistency lets readers reason by analogy","tool":"craft","text":"03 Consistency lets readers reason by analogy Consistency — same concept, same name; same task, same pattern; conventions everywhere — slashes cognitive load and shrinks unknown unknowns. If user_id always means the same thing, parameters always go in the same order, and one pattern always handles e","keywords":["consistency","lets","readers","reason","by","analogy","user_id"]},{"url":"craft/names-consistency-obvious.html#obvious-code","page":"Names, consistency & obvious code","section":"The test for “obvious”","tool":"craft","text":"04 The test for “obvious” Code is obvious when a reader grasps it quickly and correctly without much thought; obscure when they must stop and puzzle. The catch: you're the worst judge of your own code, because you already hold the context in your head. Red flag gotcha “It's obvious to me .” If a rev","keywords":["the","test","for","obvious"]},{"url":"craft/names-consistency-obvious.html#check","page":"Names, consistency & obvious code","section":"Check yourself","tool":"craft","text":"05 Check yourself A reviewer says they can't tell what tmp holds in your function. Your move? Rename it to something precise — their confusion means the code is obscure. Explain it in the PR thread and move on; the name is fine. Add # tmp is temporary . Rename it. Reader confusion is the definition ","keywords":["check","yourself","tmp","is","temporary"]},{"url":"judgment/design-it-twice-and-trends.html","page":"Design it twice · trends · performance","section":"Design it twice · trends · performance.","tool":"judgment","text":"Design it twice · trends · performance. A grab-bag of hard-won judgment. The cheapest way to get a better design is to invent two; the popular trends (OOP, agile, TDD, patterns) are tools, not gospel; and performance, done right, almost never fights clean design.","keywords":["design","it","twice","trends","performance"]},{"url":"judgment/design-it-twice-and-trends.html#design-it-twice","page":"Design it twice · trends · performance","section":"Design it twice","tool":"judgment","text":"01 Design it twice Before committing, sketch at least two genuinely different designs and compare them. Even experts rarely nail the best structure on the first try. In plain English analogy It's trying on two outfits before leaving the house. You learn more from the contrast in thirty seconds than ","keywords":["design","it","twice"]},{"url":"judgment/design-it-twice-and-trends.html#trends","page":"Design it twice · trends · performance","section":"Trends, judged by complexity","tool":"judgment","text":"02 Trends, judged by complexity Ousterhout asks one question of every fashion: does it reduce complexity here, or am I cargo-culting it? Trend Use it for Watch out OOP & inheritance Interface inheritance & composition. Implementation inheritance couples parent & child — prefer composition. Agile Bui","keywords":["trends","judged","by","complexity"]},{"url":"judgment/design-it-twice-and-trends.html#ai-era","page":"Design it twice · trends · performance","section":"AI-assisted coding, judged the same way","tool":"judgment","text":"03 AI-assisted coding, judged the same way AI changes who types the code , not what design is for. Ask the same question of it as of every trend: does it reduce complexity here? In plain English analogy A coding assistant is a brilliant new teammate with no memory of your codebase — every session, i","keywords":["ai","assisted","coding","judged","the","same","way"]},{"url":"judgment/design-it-twice-and-trends.html#performance","page":"Design it twice · trends · performance","section":"Designing for performance","tool":"judgment","text":"04 Designing for performance Clean design is usually fast enough. Don't trade clarity for speculative speed — and when you must optimise, measure first , then simplify the critical path. The instinct to micro-optimise everywhere injects complexity for gains that don't matter. The disciplined move: p","keywords":["designing","for","performance"]},{"url":"judgment/design-it-twice-and-trends.html#check","page":"Design it twice · trends · performance","section":"Check yourself","tool":"judgment","text":"05 Check yourself You have a working first design. What does “design it twice” recommend? Sketch a genuinely different second design and compare trade-offs before committing. Ship the first one — it works, so it's done. Rewrite the same design with nicer names. Compare a different second design. The","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"judgment/red-flags-playbook.html","page":"The red-flags playbook","section":"The red-flags playbook.","tool":"judgment","text":"The red-flags playbook. The whole book in two lists. The principles are what to aim for; the red flags are the smells that tell you complexity is creeping in. Learn to spot a red flag mid-edit and you'll catch bad design before it sets.","keywords":["the","red","flags","playbook"]},{"url":"judgment/red-flags-playbook.html#the-one-rule","page":"The red-flags playbook","section":"The one rule under all the rules","tool":"judgment","text":"01 The one rule under all the rules In plain English analogy Every red flag is a smoke detector for the same fire: complexity . You don't need to memorise fifteen rules — you need to notice smoke and ask “what made this harder to understand, and how do I undo it?” It's all about complexity. The prin","keywords":["the","one","rule","under","all","rules"]},{"url":"judgment/red-flags-playbook.html#principles","page":"The red-flags playbook","section":"The design principles","tool":"judgment","text":"02 The design principles 01 · Complexity is incremental It accrues a grain at a time — fight it continually, not in cleanup sprints. → 02 · Be strategic Working code isn't enough; invest ~10–20% in design. → 03 · Make modules deep Simple interface, powerful implementation. → 04 · Hide information Bu","keywords":["the","design","principles"]},{"url":"judgment/red-flags-playbook.html#red-flags","page":"The red-flags playbook","section":"The red flags","tool":"judgment","text":"03 The red flags Red flag What you'll see Fix lives in Shallow module Interface almost as complex as the implementation — little hidden. Deep modules Information leakage One design decision shows up in two+ modules; they change in lockstep. Information hiding Temporal decomposition Module structure ","keywords":["the","red","flags"]},{"url":"judgment/red-flags-playbook.html#spot-them","page":"The red-flags playbook","section":"Spot them live","tool":"judgment","text":"04 Spot them live Reading the table is one thing; noticing the smell in real code mid-review is the skill. Three rounds — click the line where the red flag lives. Reveal Next round Reset Click the smelly line · / + Enter works too · -> for the next round once solved.","keywords":["spot","them","live"]},{"url":"judgment/red-flags-playbook.html#check","page":"The red-flags playbook","section":"Check yourself","tool":"judgment","text":"05 Check yourself You can't think of a good name for a method, and a short comment won't capture what it does. What's that telling you? It's a red flag — the underlying design is muddled; fix the design, not the label. Nothing — naming is just hard sometimes. Use a long descriptive comment and move ","keywords":["check","yourself"]},{"url":"reference/red-flags.html","page":"Red-flags cheatsheet","section":"Red-flags cheatsheet.","tool":"reference","text":"Red-flags cheatsheet. Every design smell from the book on one page, each with a one-line fix and the principle behind it. Skim before a code review or when something “feels” complex but you can't name why.","keywords":["red","flags","cheatsheet"]},{"url":"reference/red-flags.html#flags","page":"Red-flags cheatsheet","section":"The red flags","tool":"reference","text":"01 The red flags Modules & abstraction Shallow module Interface ≈ implementation. → Make it deeper, or merge it. Information leakage One decision in 2+ modules. → Hide it behind a single module. Temporal decomposition Structure follows execution order. → Organise by knowledge, not timing. Overexposu","keywords":["the","red","flags","tmp","data","do_it"]},{"url":"reference/red-flags.html#principles","page":"Red-flags cheatsheet","section":"Principles at a glance","tool":"reference","text":"02 Principles at a glance Mindset It's all about complexity The thing every rule is fighting. Complexity is incremental Fight it continually, a grain at a time. Be strategic Working code isn't enough — invest ~10–20% in design. Design it twice Two real alternatives beat the first idea. Modules Make ","keywords":["principles","at","glance"]},{"url":"reference/flashcards.html","page":"Flashcards","section":"Flashcards.","tool":"reference","text":"Flashcards. The principles and red flags as recall practice. Click a card to flip; mark it Known or Review; Shuffle floats the ones you don't know yet to the front. Progress saves in your browser.","keywords":["flashcards"]},{"url":"reference/flashcards.html#deck","page":"Flashcards","section":"The deck","tool":"reference","text":"01 The deck 0 / 0 known Shuffle Reset What is complexity? Anything about a system's structure that makes it hard to understand or modify. It's structural and incremental. Known Review The three symptoms of complexity Change amplification, cognitive load, and unknown unknowns (the worst — you can't t","keywords":["the","deck"]}];
